  "id": "DrugBank_DB06595 - MONDO_15667",
  "verbalization": "Midostaurin is associated with Unclassified Acute Myeloid Leukemia through a series of indication pathways, with the core mechanism likely involving the inhibition of specific molecular targets. The evidence suggests that Midostaurin may be repurposed for this leukemia subtype, as it is indicated in cases with specific genetic alterations, such as Inv3(p21;q26.2) or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2), which are associated with the disease. These genetic markers may indicate a potential for targeted therapy, as Midostaurin is known to inhibit certain kinases, potentially affecting the pathways involved in leukemogenesis. The shared indication pathway from Midostaurin to Ivosidenib, which in turn is indicated for Unclassified Acute Myeloid Leukemia, further supports a mechanistic link. The presence of multiple pathways and the consistent scores suggest that the relationship is supported by a combination of clinical and molecular evidence, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
